<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Plataea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/479-bce">479 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Plataea · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Plataea was fought in 479 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, where an allied Greek army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> defeated the Persian land forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a>. It ended the second Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a>. The defeat marked the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>'s attempts to conquer mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea.jpg" alt="Battle of Plataea" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Steeple Davis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scene_of_the_Battle_of_Plataea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> began preparing an invasion of Greece soon after his accession in 486 BCE, and a congress of city states, probably at Corinth, formed an alliance known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in 481 BCE. In August 480 BCE a small Allied army under the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a> held the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> for three days before being outflanked. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> then occupied the evacuated city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The Allied navy won a decisive victory at Salamis, and Xerxes retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> with a small part of his army, leaving his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> to finish the conquest the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> retreated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, hoping to draw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> onto ground suited to his cavalry, and built a fortified camp on the north bank of the Asopos river. The Greek army, commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a>, the regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-cithaeron">Mount Cithaeron</a> and took positions on high ground opposite. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> the Spartans sent 45,000 men, among them 5,000 full citizens and 35,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>. Estimates of the Persian army, drawn from the size of its camp, range from about 70,000 to 120,000, though modern historians differ widely.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> refused to descend to the plain, and a stalemate lasted eleven days. Persian cavalry then cut the Greek supply lines. On the thirteenth day the Greeks tried to withdraw, and their line broke up. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> took this for a full retreat and ordered a pursuit. The Spartans, Tegeans and Athenians turned and fought, routed the lightly armed Persian infantry and killed Mardonius. Much of the Persian army was trapped in its camp and killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale">Battle of Mycale</a> was fought on the same day, destroying what remained of the Persian fleet. After the two victories the second Persian invasion was over, and the Persian desire to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> gradually diminished. The sale of the loot paid for buildings such as the Athenian Treasury at Delphi.</p><p>A bronze column of intertwined snakes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpent-column">Serpent Column</a>, was made from melted Persian armour and set up at Delphi, listing the 31 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> that fought; most of it now stands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippodrome-of-constantinople">Hippodrome of Constantinople</a>. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides of Ceos</a> wrote an elegy and an encomium on the battle, and in Spartan tradition the victory avenged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Plataea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Greek Wars showed the limits of Persian arms against determined Greek hoplites and fleets, at Marathon (490 BCE), Salamis (480 BCE) and <b>Plataea</b> (479 BCE).</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the urging of Themistocles the city then built a fleet of some 200 triremes, which formed the core of the Greek navy that defeated Xerxes I at the Battle of Salamis in 480 BCE, and Athenians fought at <b>Plataea</b> in 479 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Boeotia, the fertile plain of central Greece between Attica and Phocis, held a dozen or more independent cities, of which Thebes was the largest, followed by Orchomenus, Thespiae, Tanagra and <b>Plataea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ionian Revolt and two Persian invasions of Greece, the Allies defeated the forces of Xerxes I at the <b>Battle of Plataea</b> and the Battle of Mycale in 479 BCE, then took Sestos and, in 478 BCE, Byzantion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…art and politics: the Athenian Treasury, the bronze Charioteer of Delphi of about 475 BCE, and the Serpent Column set up after the <b>Battle of Plataea</b> in 479 BCE, which Constantine the Great later moved to Constantinople, where its remains still stand in the old Hippodrome of Istanbul.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Macedonia was never made a satrapy, but it supplied troops: Alexander I supported Xerxes I in 480–479 BCE, and Macedonians fought on the Persian side at the <b>Battle of Plataea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
